    date: "07-28-26",
    text: `Finished school for the semester! As long as I get a 66% on my ethics final and a 71% on my English final I’ll keep my 4.0 GPA. I’m 98% confident these two both happened. I already submitted all the work needed, just waiting on grades to come in. Summer semester is fun. I like the rushed semester. Not as much wasted time in class dilly dallying. More credits quicker. It’s very focused which makes it easy to not get off track or lazy because there’s almost always an assignment to be working on. It’ll be interesting to watch how college and learning shifts over the next few years as I’m in school. I think more and more professors are going to realize that conducting a college class in 1990, 2010, and 2026 are all going to look very different. Less tech and more handwritten notes I think is the way. AI can pretty much do any homework assignment nowadays such as write an essay, fill out a worksheet, or conduct research for a topic. So I think it may be beneficial to have more test based formats in subjects that need specific knowledge acquired and having more discussion based classes with less or minimal assignments entirely to encourage engagement and usage of the brain and not using AI. AI makes things a lot more efficient and easier, but it also reduces some of that key function and pondering that we have evolved to use. I liked that one study from MIT on your brain on AI. https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08872 . Using AI can affect memory recall, strategic idea integration, reduced brain connectivity and lower engagement. It’s like almost a trade off. It can write and synthesize information 10x faster, and arguably often better. But then you neglect a ton of pathways in the brain that used to make these connections. It’s like riding a bike everywhere and then suddenly you get a car. Now you never use those bike legs. Sure you’re faster and can accomplish more. But the value of biking (physically and emotionally) may actually outweigh the efficiency at times of the car. Efficiency and just getting the job done may not always be the most useful thing. In fact, going out too fast or eliminating some of the process can create downsides even If not seen immediately. I don’t pay for AI anymore. I think that’s nice. I have been trying to do more learning from books where I practice recall and learning the classic way where information was a little bit harder to come by, especially specifics are harder to dig for. But I actually feel the brain used more. Whereas AI is such an instant answer machine to many specific questions it removes that process of gaining some basic general information and then slowly putting the puzzle pieces together to get to a conclusion. While I agree AI is sort of the future and will help many things, I try to approach it like: If I don’t need to use it, don’t. Funny enough, it’s tempting to ask AI to summarize that study and report its key points and show what it displays. But there’s something cool that my gut likes about exploring it myself and thinking about how I respond and interpret the values and conclusions and how the study worked. The work of doing that is just internally good. Yes it’s less efficient. Yes I have to take longer to discover what I want. But I want that.`
